{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And higher than Agag shall be his kingdom</b> — Their first king will conquer Agag king of Amalek. His name is called before he is born; it is similar to \"to Cyrus whose right hand I have held\" (Isaiah 45:1), \"Behold, a son shall be born to the house of David, Josiah by name\" (I Kings 13:2), and Isaac (Genesis 17:19), and Ishmael (ibid. 16:11), and Solomon (I Chronicles 22:9). \"And his kingdom shall be exalted\" — the kingdom of Jacob more and more, for David and Solomon his son will come after him. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 24:7), and he interpreted well. And it is possible that every king among the people of Amalek was called Agag, for the first king they set over themselves was so named, and his sons who sat on his throne were called by his name, after the manner of most kings even today who are called by the name of those who hold the kingdom. And likewise \"Haman the Agagite\" (Esther 8:3), for he was of that royal seed, for it is far-fetched that the prophet would call the name of the wicked one before he was formed in the womb. And likewise \"Gog\" (Ezekiel 39:1) — every prince of Magog will be called thus. Now in this third time he added to inform Balak that the tents of Jacob were good, alluding to the time when they were dwellers in tents until they would inherit the land, and the dwelling-places of Israel would also be good after conquest and division, when they would dwell in it in secure dwelling-places. And likewise his land would be filled with every good like a watered garden and like a spring of water whose waters would not fail, and that he would defeat Amalek on whose account he fought and blot out his memory, and the kingdom of Israel would be exalted further, for afterward mighty kings would arise in it, lifted up exceedingly. And he told him that he would eat all his enemies and crush their bones, alluding to their wealth and all that is theirs. And he said that they would dwell in it in safety, they would not fear any people, like a lion and a lioness that does not fear beasts of prey. Now Balak despaired of him, for if he fought against him he would truly perish, for he would eat all his enemies as he would eat Amalek on whose account he fought. Therefore he said to him now that he should flee to his place — he has no desire for him."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:08:26.542995607Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}